  # Why: An adversarial Player output could embed instructions inside a
  #       <think> block designed to leak through if the stripping helper
  #       is naive — e.g. nested tags or content that re-wraps after the
  #       strip. Defence-in-depth: post-strip output must contain no
  #       <think> markers at all, even after multiple strip passes.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] The stripping helper is idempotent —
  #       re-running it on already-stripped content produces no change
  #       (ASSUM-008).
  @task:TASK-RAG05-002
  @edge-case @security
  Scenario: Stripping helper is idempotent against re-injection attempts
    Given the Player produced output where stripping <think> blocks once still leaves a <think> token in the result
    When the orchestrator runs the stripping helper twice on the output
    Then the final content sent to the Coach should contain no <think> or </think> token
    And the helper should not loop indefinitely

  # Why: If an attacker (or a misconfigured installer) replaces the
  #       trained prompt with a different one, the FEAT-RAG-07 thesis
  #       result becomes meaningless — the model is no longer being run
  #       against what it was trained on. Operator must be able to
  #       verify the active prompt artefact.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=low] The trained prompt artefact's identity
  #       is recorded in the session log (e.g. its source path or a
  #       content hash) so post-hoc audits can verify which prompt was
  #       actually used in a given session (ASSUM-009).
  @task:TASK-RAG05-001
  @edge-case @security
  Scenario: Active trained-prompt artefact identity is recorded in the session log
    Given AGENT_MODELS__REASONING_MODEL is set to "local"
    When the orchestrator resolves and uses the Player system prompt
    Then the session log should record which prompt artefact was loaded
    And an operator should be able to verify the artefact identity from the log alone

  # Why: Two operators (or a fleet topology — see NATS fleet integration)
  #       may run sessions concurrently with different reasoning_model
  #       settings. Prompt selection must not depend on global mutable
  #       state — each session resolves independently from its own
  #       configuration.
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=medium] Each session's prompt is resolved
  #       from the configuration in scope at session-start, not from
  #       any process-wide cache that could be mutated by a later
  #       session (ASSUM-010).
  @task:TASK-RAG05-001
  @edge-case
  Scenario: Concurrent sessions with different providers resolve prompts independently
    Given session A is started with AGENT_MODELS__REASONING_MODEL set to "local"
    And session B is started with AGENT_MODELS__REASONING_MODEL set to "claude"
    When both sessions resolve their Player system prompts at the same time
    Then session A's prompt should be the GOAL.md-trained architect prompt
    And session B's prompt should be the existing Phase 0 architect Player prompt
    And changing the environment variable in one session should not affect the other

  # Why: Architect role has four Player prompt variants (greenfield,
